TI  - PROLONGED EFFECTS OF MATERNAL ALCOHOL INGESTION ON THE NEONATAL ELECTROENCEPHALOGRAM
T2  - PEDIATRICS
LA  - English
AB  - Newborn infants of mothers who drink heavily (> 2 oz alcohol/day) during pregnancy were shown to have hypersynchrony of the EEG. The possibility that hypersynchrony is related to acute alcohol withdrawal was tested. Preterm infants (11) of mothers who drank heavily (alcoholic) were studied at a postnatal age of 4-6 wk using power spectral analysis of EEG signals. This group was compared with a control group with similar racial and socioeconomic status. In addition, a group of 10 healthy  preterm infants was compared with a carefully matched control group. Total power of the EEG during quiet, indeterminate and rapid eye movement (REM) sleep was 162, 183 and 188%, respectively, in the infants whose mothers were alcoholic when compared with their control infants or with the healthy preterm infants and their control infants. Ingestion of alcohol during pregnancy may result in potentially serious prolonged effects on brain function of the offspring, even in the absence of dysmorphology.
