LA  - English
AB  - Patients (29) with growth hormone (GH) deficiency were selected according to the following criteria: no evidence of reversible GH deficiency, onset of growth retardation in early childhood, and no evidence of pituitary tumors or other direct pituitary trauma. Of the patients, 14 had evidence of multiple hormone deficiencies, 14 had isolated GH deficiency, and 1 patient questionable isolated GH deficiency. Psychometric testing showed a normal IQ distribution. The GH deficiency was not associated with deficiencies in specific mental abilities and GH treatment in later childhood and adolescence did not influence intelligence. Patients with multiple hormone deficiencies had somewhat lower IQs than patients with isolated GH deficiency when socioeconomic status was controlled. GH deficiency apparently does not affect human brain development and intelligence.
