AB  - Background This study aims to identify the contexts, environments, and nurturing care predictors that determine whether a child is developmentally on track in Ceara, Brazil.
   Methods We analysed data from a cross-sectional study conducted with caregivers of 6,447 children aged 0-59 months during a vaccination campaign in Ceara in October 2019. The validated Child Development Assessment Questionnaire was used to assess early childhood development (ECD) and children with a z-score >= - 1 SD were considered developmentally on track. We conducted logistic regression models to understand the effects of contexts, environments, and nurturing care domains on ECD.
   Findings Children in the early years (< 36 months) were more likely to meet the ECD milestones if they were not born with low birth weight (AOR: 0 cent 64; 95% CI: 0 center dot 42-0 center dot 97), were exposed to manufactured toys in their house (2 center dot 68; 1 center dot 97-3 center dot 66), their heads of household were employed (1 center dot 61; 1 center dot 16-2 center dot 23), and their caregivers had read the Child Health Handbook (1 center dot 42; 1 center dot 13-1 center dot 77) and engaged them in stimulating activities (1 center dot 71; 1 center dot 26-2 center dot 32). Children aged 36 - 59 months were more likely to meet the ECD milestones if they were breastfed (never: ref. / < 3 months: 3 center dot 72; 1 center dot 91 -7 center dot 26 / 3-5 months: 3 center dot 21; 1 center dot 74-5 center dot 93 / 6-11 months: 3 center dot 73; 1 center dot 95-7 center dot 16 / >= 12 months: 3 center dot 89; 2 center dot 25-6 center dot 72), had books at home (0: ref /1-3: 1 center dot 71; 1 center dot 22-2 center dot 40 / 4-6: 2 center dot 24; 1 center dot 27-3 center dot 94 / 7+: 2 center dot 71; 1 center dot 05-7 center dot 00), and their caregivers received information about ECD (1 center dot 49; 1 center dot 11-2 center dot 01) and engaged them in stimulating activities (1 center dot 80; 1 center dot 27-2 center dot 56). Children aged 36-59 months were less likely to meet developmental milestones if they watched TV or used tablets/smartphones for more than two hours per day (0 center dot 61; 0 center dot 44-0 center dot 84), played with household objects (0 center dot 62; 0 center dot 41-0 center dot 92), participated in governmental early childhood programmes aimed at vulnerable families (0 center dot 62; 0 center dot 45-0 center dot 86), had families that participated in income transfer programmes (0 center dot 68; 0 center dot 47-0 center dot 99) (families living in poverty or extreme poverty), and their caregivers considered slapping (0 center dot 67; 0 center dot 48-0 center dot 94) a necessary disciplinary method.
   Interpretation Having favourable socioeconomic conditions, breastfeeding, the absence of harsh discipline, caregivers who provide responsive care, and the provision of opportunities for early learning are the key factors that increase the likelihood of a child achieving their full developmental potential in Ceara, Brazil.
